{
 "schemaVersion": 1,
 "id": "item_generation_grammar",
 "kind": "staged_proposal",
 "status": "PROPOSAL — staged, ready to drop in when CCode adds the `item` gen type.",
 "note": "⚠️ THE GRAMMAR, NOT THE ITEMS. Erik: 'fire the item creation engine to fill in weapons, armor, shields etc. Each domain and people could have some flavor of these.' This supplies what a generator draws from so its output BELONGS — a generated Ashwarden blade should read as ashwarden and a Churnfolk one should not. THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN GENERATED AND PROCEDURAL.",
 "dependsOn": "⚠️ TRACK B. `bonusTags` are currently SET and never MATCHED — nothing reads them against an action. Until the matcher lands, every item this produces is flavour text. The grammar is safe to author now; it should not SHIP before its consumer.",
 "howItComposes": {
  "formula": "{finish} {form} of {people}  ·  or  {people}'s {form}  ·  or  the {material} {form}",
  "fields": "a generated item fills: id, name, kind, description, bonusTags, consumable:false — the live schema, nothing new.",
  "bonusTagRule": "⚠️ TAGS COME FROM THE TRADITION, NOT THE FORM. A blade is `melee`+`edge` whoever made it; what makes it ASHWARDEN is `death`+`quiet`. That way the matcher rewards carrying gear that suits your craft, which is the point.",
  "tierRule": "kind alone does not set power. A generated item is `tier` 1-3 from the maker's standing; the grammar supplies flavour, not numbers."
 },
